# Setup

Once the [](prerequisites.md) are installed setup is simple enough.

```sh
git clone {{{repoUrl}}}.git
cd {{{repoName}}}
pnpm install
```

`pnpm install` also installs the git hooks. The checks, commit message rules and release process are described in
`CONTRIBUTING.md`.

## Running a development Foundry VTT

The compose file in `.foundryvtt/` runs the pinned Foundry VTT release (`VERSION` in `.foundryvtt/.env`) with this
project mounted as the `{{{moduleId}}}` module.

1. Copy `.foundryvtt/secrets.example.json` to `.foundryvtt/secrets.json` and fill in your foundryvtt.com credentials,
   your licence key and an admin password for the server. The file is ignored by {program}`git`.
2. Build the module once, so that Foundry finds the bundle named in `module.json`:

   ```sh
   pnpm build
   ```

3. Start Foundry VTT on <http://localhost:30000>:

   ```sh
   pnpm serve:vtt
   ```

4. Start the \{{HMR}} development server:

   ```sh
   pnpm dev
   ```

   Open <http://localhost:31002> rather than port 30000. The development server serves the module straight from `src/`
   and passes everything else on to Foundry VTT. To reach it under another host name, list the name in the
   `VITE_ALLOWED_HOSTS` environment variable (comma-separated).

## The module manifest

`module.json` was filled in when the project was generated. Review:

`title` and `description`
: These appear on Foundry's Add-on Modules page. Keep the description short.

`authors`
: Provide at least one author entry.

`compatibility`
: Which versions of Foundry this module is compatible with.

`relationships`
: Any game systems and modules this module requires.

You may also need to set various other values in `module.json` (e.g. `socket` if your module uses sockets), but
these will vary widely according to what your module does.

If you use {program}`github` for your versioning and distribution then you can leave the following entries alone and
the release workflow will maintain them for you. (Otherwise, see [](#replacing-github).)

- `version`
- `manifest`
- `download`

## Repository settings

The workflows in `.github/workflows` need:

`PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN`
: A repository secret holding a token that may push to `main`; the release workflow commits the changelog and the
  stamped `module.json` with it.

`FOUNDRY_USERNAME`, `FOUNDRY_PASSWORD`, `FOUNDRY_LICENSE_KEY` and `FOUNDRY_ADMIN_KEY`
: Repository secrets used by the end-to-end tests to download and license a Foundry VTT server.

GitHub Pages
: Set to deploy from the `gh-pages` branch, which the documentation workflow maintains.
